{
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "ThreatForge",
          "version": "1.0.0",
          "informationUri": "https://github.com/rmr369/threatforge-threatmodel",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "TF-CMP-001",
              "name": "ComposeServiceRunsPrivileged",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Compose service runs privileged"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "ComposeService api runs with `privileged: true`, disabling container isolation entirely. Code execution in this service is equivalent to root on the host."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "ComposeService api runs with `privileged: true`, disabling container isolation entirely. Code execution in this service is equivalent to root on the host. **Remediation:** Remove privileged; grant specific cap_add entries instead. ```yaml privileged: false cap_drop: [\"ALL\"] cap_add: [\"NET_BIND_SERVICE\"] ``` CWE: CWE-250 \u00b7 MITRE: T1611 \u00b7 NIST: AC-6",
                "markdown": "ComposeService api runs with `privileged: true`, disabling container isolation entirely. Code execution in this service is equivalent to root on the host.\n\n**Remediation:** Remove privileged; grant specific cap_add entries instead.\n\n```yaml\nprivileged: false\ncap_drop: [\"ALL\"]\ncap_add: [\"NET_BIND_SERVICE\"]\n```\n\nCWE: CWE-250 \u00b7 MITRE: T1611 \u00b7 NIST: AC-6"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compose",
                  "critical-path",
                  "cwe/CWE-250",
                  "mitre/T1611",
                  "pack:docker-build",
                  "privilege",
                  "security",
                  "stride/E",
                  "stride/T"
                ],
                "security-severity": "10.0",
                "precision": "very-high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-CMP-002",
              "name": "ComposeServiceMountsTheDockerSocket",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Compose service mounts the Docker socket"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "ComposeService api mounts /var/run/docker.sock. This grants full control of the Docker daemon, which is root on the host by another name."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "ComposeService api mounts /var/run/docker.sock. This grants full control of the Docker daemon, which is root on the host by another name. **Remediation:** Use a socket proxy with a minimal API allowlist, or rootless Docker. CWE: CWE-250, CWE-284 \u00b7 MITRE: T1610 \u00b7 NIST: AC-6",
                "markdown": "ComposeService api mounts /var/run/docker.sock. This grants full control of the Docker daemon, which is root on the host by another name.\n\n**Remediation:** Use a socket proxy with a minimal API allowlist, or rootless Docker.\n\nCWE: CWE-250, CWE-284 \u00b7 MITRE: T1610 \u00b7 NIST: AC-6"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compose",
                  "container-escape",
                  "critical-path",
                  "cwe/CWE-250",
                  "cwe/CWE-284",
                  "mitre/T1610",
                  "pack:docker-build",
                  "security",
                  "stride/E",
                  "stride/I",
                  "stride/T"
                ],
                "security-severity": "10.0",
                "precision": "very-high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-CMP-004",
              "name": "ComposeServiceHasPlaintextCredentialsInEnvironment",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Compose service has plaintext credentials in environment"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Credential-shaped environment variables are set inline in the compose file, which is version-controlled and visible to anyone who can run `docker inspect`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Credential-shaped environment variables are set inline in the compose file, which is version-controlled and visible to anyone who can run `docker inspect`. **Remediation:** Move values to an untracked env_file or to Docker/Swarm secrets. ```yaml env_file: [.env] # add .env to .gitignore # or, in swarm mode: # secrets: [db_password] ``` CWE: CWE-798 \u00b7 MITRE: T1552.001 \u00b7 NIST: IA-5",
                "markdown": "Credential-shaped environment variables are set inline in the compose file, which is version-controlled and visible to anyone who can run `docker inspect`.\n\n**Remediation:** Move values to an untracked env_file or to Docker/Swarm secrets.\n\n```yaml\nenv_file: [.env]      # add .env to .gitignore\n# or, in swarm mode:\n# secrets: [db_password]\n```\n\nCWE: CWE-798 \u00b7 MITRE: T1552.001 \u00b7 NIST: IA-5"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compose",
                  "cwe/CWE-798",
                  "mitre/T1552.001",
                  "pack:docker-build",
                  "secrets",
                  "security",
                  "stride/I"
                ],
                "security-severity": "10.0",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-K8S-001",
              "name": "ContainerRunsInPrivilegedMode",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Container runs in privileged mode"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Container shop/web runs with `privileged: true`, which disables essentially every container isolation control: all Linux capabilities are granted, /dev is fully exposed, and AppArmor/seccomp are effectively bypassed. An attacker with code execution in this container can mount the host filesystem, write to /etc/kubernetes, read the kubelet credentials, and take over the node -- and from the node, every other pod scheduled on it."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Container shop/web runs with `privileged: true`, which disables essentially every container isolation control: all Linux capabilities are granted, /dev is fully exposed, and AppArmor/seccomp are effectively bypassed. An attacker with code execution in this container can mount the host filesystem, write to /etc/kubernetes, read the kubelet credentials, and take over the node -- and from the node, every other pod scheduled on it. **Remediation:** Remove privileged mode and grant only the specific capabilities needed. Identify why privilege was needed. The usual culprits are raw sockets (NET_RAW), binding to ports below 1024 (NET_BIND_SERVICE), or device access. Grant those capabilities individually. If the workload genuinely needs host access (CNI plugin, node exporter), isolate it on dedicated nodes with taints and document the exception. ```yaml securityContext: privileged: false allowPrivilegeEscalation: false capabilities: drop: [\"ALL\"] add: [] # add back only what is proven necessary ``` CWE: CWE-250, CWE-269 \u00b7 MITRE: T1611, T1610 \u00b7 CIS: 5.2.1 \u00b7 NIST: AC-6, CM-7 \u00b7 OWASP: K01",
                "markdown": "Container shop/web runs with `privileged: true`, which disables essentially every container isolation control: all Linux capabilities are granted, /dev is fully exposed, and AppArmor/seccomp are effectively bypassed. An attacker with code execution in this container can mount the host filesystem, write to /etc/kubernetes, read the kubelet credentials, and take over the node -- and from the node, every other pod scheduled on it.\n\n**Remediation:** Remove privileged mode and grant only the specific capabilities needed.\n\nIdentify why privilege was needed. The usual culprits are raw sockets (NET_RAW), binding to ports below 1024 (NET_BIND_SERVICE), or device access. Grant those capabilities individually. If the workload genuinely needs host access (CNI plugin, node exporter), isolate it on dedicated nodes with taints and document the exception.\n\n\n```yaml\nsecurityContext:\n  privileged: false\n  allowPrivilegeEscalation: false\n  capabilities:\n    drop: [\"ALL\"]\n    add: []            # add back only what is proven necessary\n```\n\nCWE: CWE-250, CWE-269 \u00b7 MITRE: T1611, T1610 \u00b7 CIS: 5.2.1 \u00b7 NIST: AC-6, CM-7 \u00b7 OWASP: K01"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "container-escape",
                  "cwe/CWE-250",
                  "cwe/CWE-269",
                  "mitre/T1610",
                  "mitre/T1611",
                  "pack:k8s-workload",
                  "privilege",
                  "security",
                  "stride/E",
                  "stride/I",
                  "stride/T"
                ],
                "security-severity": "10.0",
                "precision": "very-high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-K8S-004",
              "name": "ContainerMountsTheContainerRuntimeSocket",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Container mounts the container runtime socket"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Deployment shop/storefront mounts the Docker/containerd/CRI-O socket. This is equivalent to granting root on the node: anyone who can write to the socket can start a new privileged container with the host filesystem mounted. There is no meaningful sandbox left."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Deployment shop/storefront mounts the Docker/containerd/CRI-O socket. This is equivalent to granting root on the node: anyone who can write to the socket can start a new privileged container with the host filesystem mounted. There is no meaningful sandbox left. **Remediation:** Remove the socket mount; use a rootless build tool or a proxy with an allowlist. For image builds use Kaniko, Buildah rootless, or BuildKit with the rootless daemon. For log/metric collection use the kubelet API or the CRI API through a dedicated read-only proxy such as docker-socket-proxy with the API surface restricted to what the agent actually needs. ```yaml # delete this volume and its volumeMount # - name: docker-sock # hostPath: # path: /var/run/docker.sock ``` CWE: CWE-250, CWE-284 \u00b7 MITRE: T1610, T1611 \u00b7 CIS: 5.2.4 \u00b7 NIST: AC-6, SC-7",
                "markdown": "Deployment shop/storefront mounts the Docker/containerd/CRI-O socket. This is equivalent to granting root on the node: anyone who can write to the socket can start a new privileged container with the host filesystem mounted. There is no meaningful sandbox left.\n\n**Remediation:** Remove the socket mount; use a rootless build tool or a proxy with an allowlist.\n\nFor image builds use Kaniko, Buildah rootless, or BuildKit with the rootless daemon. For log/metric collection use the kubelet API or the CRI API through a dedicated read-only proxy such as docker-socket-proxy with the API surface restricted to what the agent actually needs.\n\n\n```yaml\n# delete this volume and its volumeMount\n# - name: docker-sock\n#   hostPath:\n#     path: /var/run/docker.sock\n```\n\nCWE: CWE-250, CWE-284 \u00b7 MITRE: T1610, T1611 \u00b7 CIS: 5.2.4 \u00b7 NIST: AC-6, SC-7"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "container-escape",
                  "critical-path",
                  "cwe/CWE-250",
                  "cwe/CWE-284",
                  "mitre/T1610",
                  "mitre/T1611",
                  "pack:k8s-workload",
                  "security",
                  "stride/E",
                  "stride/I",
                  "stride/T"
                ],
                "security-severity": "10.0",
                "precision": "very-high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-K8S-015",
              "name": "PlaintextCredentialInContainerEnvironmentVariables",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Plaintext credential in container environment variables"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Environment variables DB_PASSWORD, STRIPE_API_KEY on Container shop/web contain literal values and are named like credentials. Env vars are visible to anyone with `kubectl describe pod`, are captured in crash dumps and in most APM/error-tracking agents, are inherited by every child process, and are readable from /proc/<pid>/environ by anything sharing the PID namespace."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Environment variables DB_PASSWORD, STRIPE_API_KEY on Container shop/web contain literal values and are named like credentials. Env vars are visible to anyone with `kubectl describe pod`, are captured in crash dumps and in most APM/error-tracking agents, are inherited by every child process, and are readable from /proc/<pid>/environ by anything sharing the PID namespace. **Remediation:** Move the value into a Secret and reference it, or mount it as a file. Files are preferable to env vars: they can be rotated without a pod restart and are not inherited by child processes. Best of all, source from an external secret manager (External Secrets Operator, Vault Agent, cloud CSI driver) so the material never lives in the cluster's etcd at all. ```yaml env: - name: DB_PASSWORD valueFrom: secretKeyRef: name: app-db key: password ``` CWE: CWE-798, CWE-522 \u00b7 MITRE: T1552.001, T1552.007 \u00b7 CIS: 5.4.1 \u00b7 NIST: IA-5, SC-28 \u00b7 OWASP: K08",
                "markdown": "Environment variables DB_PASSWORD, STRIPE_API_KEY on Container shop/web contain literal values and are named like credentials. Env vars are visible to anyone with `kubectl describe pod`, are captured in crash dumps and in most APM/error-tracking agents, are inherited by every child process, and are readable from /proc/<pid>/environ by anything sharing the PID namespace.\n\n**Remediation:** Move the value into a Secret and reference it, or mount it as a file.\n\nFiles are preferable to env vars: they can be rotated without a pod restart and are not inherited by child processes. Best of all, source from an external secret manager (External Secrets Operator, Vault Agent, cloud CSI driver) so the material never lives in the cluster's etcd at all.\n\n\n```yaml\nenv:\n  - name: DB_PASSWORD\n    valueFrom:\n      secretKeyRef:\n        name: app-db\n        key: password\n```\n\nCWE: CWE-798, CWE-522 \u00b7 MITRE: T1552.001, T1552.007 \u00b7 CIS: 5.4.1 \u00b7 NIST: IA-5, SC-28 \u00b7 OWASP: K08"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "critical-path",
                  "cwe/CWE-522",
                  "cwe/CWE-798",
                  "mitre/T1552.001",
                  "mitre/T1552.007",
                  "pack:k8s-workload",
                  "secrets",
                  "security",
                  "stride/I",
                  "stride/S"
                ],
                "security-severity": "10.0",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-NET-003",
              "name": "IngressDoesNotEnforceTls",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Ingress does not enforce TLS"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Ingress shop/storefront-ingress accepts traffic without TLS. Credentials, session cookies, and response data cross the public internet in cleartext, and there is nothing to stop an on-path attacker rewriting responses to serve malicious JavaScript."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Ingress shop/storefront-ingress accepts traffic without TLS. Credentials, session cookies, and response data cross the public internet in cleartext, and there is nothing to stop an on-path attacker rewriting responses to serve malicious JavaScript. **Remediation:** Add a TLS block and force HTTPS redirection. ```yaml metadata: annotations: nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/force-ssl-redirect: \"true\" cert-manager.io/cluster-issuer: letsencrypt-prod spec: tls: - hosts: [\"app.example.com\"] secretName: app-tls ``` CWE: CWE-319, CWE-311 \u00b7 MITRE: T1040, T1557 \u00b7 CIS: 5.3.1 \u00b7 NIST: SC-8, SC-13 \u00b7 OWASP: K05",
                "markdown": "Ingress shop/storefront-ingress accepts traffic without TLS. Credentials, session cookies, and response data cross the public internet in cleartext, and there is nothing to stop an on-path attacker rewriting responses to serve malicious JavaScript.\n\n**Remediation:** Add a TLS block and force HTTPS redirection.\n\n```yaml\nmetadata:\n  annotations:\n    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/force-ssl-redirect: \"true\"\n    cert-manager.io/cluster-issuer: letsencrypt-prod\nspec:\n  tls:\n    - hosts: [\"app.example.com\"]\n      secretName: app-tls\n```\n\nCWE: CWE-319, CWE-311 \u00b7 MITRE: T1040, T1557 \u00b7 CIS: 5.3.1 \u00b7 NIST: SC-8, SC-13 \u00b7 OWASP: K05"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "critical-path",
                  "cwe/CWE-311",
                  "cwe/CWE-319",
                  "mitre/T1040",
                  "mitre/T1557",
                  "pack:k8s-network",
                  "security",
                  "stride/I",
                  "stride/S",
                  "stride/T",
                  "transport"
                ],
                "security-severity": "10.0",
                "precision": "very-high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-NET-008",
              "name": "ServiceExposesAnAdministrativeOrDatabasePort",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Service exposes an administrative or database port"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Service shop/postgres exposes port(s) 5432 and is reachable from outside the cluster. Database, cache, and management ports (5432, 3306, 6379, 9200, 27017, 2375, 10250, 2379...) are routinely scanned; several of these protocols have no authentication enabled by default."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Service shop/postgres exposes port(s) 5432 and is reachable from outside the cluster. Database, cache, and management ports (5432, 3306, 6379, 9200, 27017, 2375, 10250, 2379...) are routinely scanned; several of these protocols have no authentication enabled by default. **Remediation:** Make the service ClusterIP and reach it through a bastion, VPN, or port-forward. ```yaml spec: type: ClusterIP # not NodePort / LoadBalancer ``` CWE: CWE-284, CWE-306 \u00b7 MITRE: T1133, T1046 \u00b7 NIST: SC-7, AC-3 \u00b7 OWASP: K05",
                "markdown": "Service shop/postgres exposes port(s) 5432 and is reachable from outside the cluster. Database, cache, and management ports (5432, 3306, 6379, 9200, 27017, 2375, 10250, 2379...) are routinely scanned; several of these protocols have no authentication enabled by default.\n\n**Remediation:** Make the service ClusterIP and reach it through a bastion, VPN, or port-forward.\n\n```yaml\nspec:\n  type: ClusterIP     # not NodePort / LoadBalancer\n```\n\nCWE: CWE-284, CWE-306 \u00b7 MITRE: T1133, T1046 \u00b7 NIST: SC-7, AC-3 \u00b7 OWASP: K05"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "critical-path",
                  "cwe/CWE-284",
                  "cwe/CWE-306",
                  "exposure",
                  "mitre/T1046",
                  "mitre/T1133",
                  "network",
                  "pack:k8s-network",
                  "security",
                  "stride/E",
                  "stride/I",
                  "stride/T"
                ],
                "security-severity": "10.0",
                "precision": "very-high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-DATA-001",
              "name": "SecretMaterialCommittedToTheRepository",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Secret material committed to the repository"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Secret shop/shop-db contains inline `data`/`stringData` in a manifest tracked in version control. base64 is an encoding, not encryption. Anyone with repository read access -- including every CI job, every fork, and the full git history after the value is \"removed\" -- has these credentials. Rotation is the only remedy once this has been pushed."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Secret shop/shop-db contains inline `data`/`stringData` in a manifest tracked in version control. base64 is an encoding, not encryption. Anyone with repository read access -- including every CI job, every fork, and the full git history after the value is \"removed\" -- has these credentials. Rotation is the only remedy once this has been pushed. **Remediation:** Rotate the credential, then source it from a secret manager. Order of operations matters: rotate first (assume it is compromised), then migrate. Use External Secrets Operator, Vault Agent Injector, Sealed Secrets, or SOPS with a KMS key. Scrub git history with git-filter-repo and force-rotate any credential that was ever pushed. ```yaml apiVersion: external-secrets.io/v1beta1 kind: ExternalSecret metadata: name: {{ name }} namespace: {{ namespace }} spec: secretStoreRef: {name: vault-backend, kind: SecretStore} target: {name: {{ name }}} data: - secretKey: password remoteRef: {key: prod/db, property: password} ``` CWE: CWE-798, CWE-540, CWE-312 \u00b7 MITRE: T1552.001, T1552.008 \u00b7 CIS: 5.4.1 \u00b7 NIST: IA-5, SC-28 \u00b7 OWASP: K08",
                "markdown": "Secret shop/shop-db contains inline `data`/`stringData` in a manifest tracked in version control. base64 is an encoding, not encryption. Anyone with repository read access -- including every CI job, every fork, and the full git history after the value is \"removed\" -- has these credentials. Rotation is the only remedy once this has been pushed.\n\n**Remediation:** Rotate the credential, then source it from a secret manager.\n\nOrder of operations matters: rotate first (assume it is compromised), then migrate. Use External Secrets Operator, Vault Agent Injector, Sealed Secrets, or SOPS with a KMS key. Scrub git history with git-filter-repo and force-rotate any credential that was ever pushed.\n\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: external-secrets.io/v1beta1\nkind: ExternalSecret\nmetadata:\n  name: {{ name }}\n  namespace: {{ namespace }}\nspec:\n  secretStoreRef: {name: vault-backend, kind: SecretStore}\n  target: {name: {{ name }}}\n  data:\n    - secretKey: password\n      remoteRef: {key: prod/db, property: password}\n```\n\nCWE: CWE-798, CWE-540, CWE-312 \u00b7 MITRE: T1552.001, T1552.008 \u00b7 CIS: 5.4.1 \u00b7 NIST: IA-5, SC-28 \u00b7 OWASP: K08"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "critical-path",
                  "cwe/CWE-312",
                  "cwe/CWE-540",
                  "cwe/CWE-798",
                  "mitre/T1552.001",
                  "mitre/T1552.008",
                  "pack:k8s-data",
                  "secrets",
                  "security",
                  "stride/I",
                  "stride/S"
                ],
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "very-high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-FLOW-001",
              "name": "UnencryptedFlowFromTheInternet",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unencrypted flow from the internet"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "ExternalEntity Internet -> Ingress shop/storefront-ingress carries traffic from an untrusted network without transport encryption. Anything on the path -- ISP, cloud provider network, a compromised intermediate -- can read and modify it."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "ExternalEntity Internet -> Ingress shop/storefront-ingress carries traffic from an untrusted network without transport encryption. Anything on the path -- ISP, cloud provider network, a compromised intermediate -- can read and modify it. **Remediation:** Terminate TLS at the edge and redirect plaintext. CWE: CWE-319 \u00b7 MITRE: T1040, T1557 \u00b7 NIST: SC-8 \u00b7 OWASP: K05",
                "markdown": "ExternalEntity Internet -> Ingress shop/storefront-ingress carries traffic from an untrusted network without transport encryption. Anything on the path -- ISP, cloud provider network, a compromised intermediate -- can read and modify it.\n\n**Remediation:** Terminate TLS at the edge and redirect plaintext.\n\nCWE: CWE-319 \u00b7 MITRE: T1040, T1557 \u00b7 NIST: SC-8 \u00b7 OWASP: K05"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "boundary",
                  "cwe/CWE-319",
                  "mitre/T1040",
                  "mitre/T1557",
                  "pack:dataflow",
                  "security",
                  "stride/I",
                  "stride/S",
                  "stride/T",
                  "transport"
                ],
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "very-high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-K8S-002",
              "name": "ContainerCanEscalatePrivileges",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Container can escalate privileges"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "allowPrivilegeEscalation is not set to false on Container shop/postgres. A setuid binary or a `sudo`-like helper inside the image can gain more privileges than its parent process, which turns a limited RCE into root inside the container and shortens the path to a container escape."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "allowPrivilegeEscalation is not set to false on Container shop/postgres. A setuid binary or a `sudo`-like helper inside the image can gain more privileges than its parent process, which turns a limited RCE into root inside the container and shortens the path to a container escape. **Remediation:** Set allowPrivilegeEscalation to false. ```yaml securityContext: allowPrivilegeEscalation: false ``` CWE: CWE-269 \u00b7 MITRE: T1548 \u00b7 CIS: 5.2.5 \u00b7 NIST: AC-6",
                "markdown": "allowPrivilegeEscalation is not set to false on Container shop/postgres. A setuid binary or a `sudo`-like helper inside the image can gain more privileges than its parent process, which turns a limited RCE into root inside the container and shortens the path to a container escape.\n\n**Remediation:** Set allowPrivilegeEscalation to false.\n\n```yaml\nsecurityContext:\n  allowPrivilegeEscalation: false\n```\n\nCWE: CWE-269 \u00b7 MITRE: T1548 \u00b7 CIS: 5.2.5 \u00b7 NIST: AC-6"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "baseline",
                  "cwe/CWE-269",
                  "mitre/T1548",
                  "pack:k8s-workload",
                  "privilege",
                  "security",
                  "stride/E"
                ],
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "very-high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-K8S-003",
              "name": "ContainerRunsAsRoot",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Container runs as root"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Container shop/postgres has no runAsNonRoot/runAsUser constraint, so it runs as UID 0 inside the container. Root in the container means write access to every file in the image, the ability to install tooling at runtime, and a materially easier escape if a kernel or runtime vulnerability is available."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Container shop/postgres has no runAsNonRoot/runAsUser constraint, so it runs as UID 0 inside the container. Root in the container means write access to every file in the image, the ability to install tooling at runtime, and a materially easier escape if a kernel or runtime vulnerability is available. **Remediation:** Run as a dedicated non-root UID. Add a non-root user in the Dockerfile, then enforce it in the manifest. Setting runAsNonRoot alone makes the kubelet refuse to start an image whose USER is root, which surfaces the problem at deploy time rather than at audit time. ```yaml securityContext: runAsNonRoot: true runAsUser: 10001 runAsGroup: 10001 ``` CWE: CWE-250 \u00b7 MITRE: T1610 \u00b7 CIS: 5.2.6 \u00b7 NIST: AC-6 \u00b7 OWASP: K01",
                "markdown": "Container shop/postgres has no runAsNonRoot/runAsUser constraint, so it runs as UID 0 inside the container. Root in the container means write access to every file in the image, the ability to install tooling at runtime, and a materially easier escape if a kernel or runtime vulnerability is available.\n\n**Remediation:** Run as a dedicated non-root UID.\n\nAdd a non-root user in the Dockerfile, then enforce it in the manifest. Setting runAsNonRoot alone makes the kubelet refuse to start an image whose USER is root, which surfaces the problem at deploy time rather than at audit time.\n\n\n```yaml\nsecurityContext:\n  runAsNonRoot: true\n  runAsUser: 10001\n  runAsGroup: 10001\n```\n\nCWE: CWE-250 \u00b7 MITRE: T1610 \u00b7 CIS: 5.2.6 \u00b7 NIST: AC-6 \u00b7 OWASP: K01"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
