            {
              "id": "TF-RBAC-001",
              "name": "ClusterroleGrantsFullClusterAdministration",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "ClusterRole grants full cluster administration"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "ClusterRole shop-operator grants `*` verbs on `*` resources cluster-wide. Any subject bound to it owns the cluster: it can read every Secret in every namespace, create privileged pods on any node, and modify admission webhooks to make the compromise persistent and invisible."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "ClusterRole shop-operator grants `*` verbs on `*` resources cluster-wide. Any subject bound to it owns the cluster: it can read every Secret in every namespace, create privileged pods on any node, and modify admission webhooks to make the compromise persistent and invisible. **Remediation:** Replace the wildcard role with an explicitly enumerated least-privilege role. Derive the real permission set from audit logs (`audit2rbac` or the API server audit log filtered by the subject) rather than guessing. Bind cluster-admin only to break-glass identities that are MFA-gated and alerted on. ```yaml rules: - apiGroups: [\"apps\"] resources: [\"deployments\"] verbs: [\"get\", \"list\", \"watch\", \"update\"] ``` CWE: CWE-269, CWE-732 \u00b7 MITRE: T1078.004, T1098 \u00b7 CIS: 5.1.1 \u00b7 NIST: AC-6, AC-2 \u00b7 OWASP: K03",
                "markdown": "ClusterRole shop-operator grants `*` verbs on `*` resources cluster-wide. Any subject bound to it owns the cluster: it can read every Secret in every namespace, create privileged pods on any node, and modify admission webhooks to make the compromise persistent and invisible.\n\n**Remediation:** Replace the wildcard role with an explicitly enumerated least-privilege role.\n\nDerive the real permission set from audit logs (`audit2rbac` or the API server audit log filtered by the subject) rather than guessing. Bind cluster-admin only to break-glass identities that are MFA-gated and alerted on.\n\n\n```yaml\nrules:\n  - apiGroups: [\"apps\"]\n    resources: [\"deployments\"]\n    verbs: [\"get\", \"list\", \"watch\", \"update\"]\n```\n\nCWE: CWE-269, CWE-732 \u00b7 MITRE: T1078.004, T1098 \u00b7 CIS: 5.1.1 \u00b7 NIST: AC-6, AC-2 \u00b7 OWASP: K03"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "critical-path",
                  "cwe/CWE-269",
                  "cwe/CWE-732",
                  "mitre/T1078.004",
                  "mitre/T1098",
                  "pack:k8s-identity",
                  "rbac",
                  "security",
                  "stride/E"
                ],
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "very-high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-CMP-003",
              "name": "ComposeServicePublishesAPortOnAllInterfaces",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Compose service publishes a port on all interfaces"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "ComposeService api publishes 8080:8080 without binding to a specific host IP, so the port is reachable on every interface -- including the public one on a cloud VM. Docker also writes iptables rules directly, which commonly bypasses host firewall configuration such as ufw."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "ComposeService api publishes 8080:8080 without binding to a specific host IP, so the port is reachable on every interface -- including the public one on a cloud VM. Docker also writes iptables rules directly, which commonly bypasses host firewall configuration such as ufw. **Remediation:** Bind to localhost and front the service with a reverse proxy. ```yaml ports: - \"127.0.0.1:8080:8080\" ``` CWE: CWE-668 \u00b7 MITRE: T1133 \u00b7 NIST: SC-7",
                "markdown": "ComposeService api publishes 8080:8080 without binding to a specific host IP, so the port is reachable on every interface -- including the public one on a cloud VM. Docker also writes iptables rules directly, which commonly bypasses host firewall configuration such as ufw.\n\n**Remediation:** Bind to localhost and front the service with a reverse proxy.\n\n```yaml\nports:\n  - \"127.0.0.1:8080:8080\"\n```\n\nCWE: CWE-668 \u00b7 MITRE: T1133 \u00b7 NIST: SC-7"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compose",
                  "cwe/CWE-668",
                  "mitre/T1133",
                  "network",
                  "pack:docker-build",
                  "security",
                  "stride/D",
                  "stride/I",
                  "stride/S"
                ],
                "security-severity": "6.4",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-K8S-006",
              "name": "PodSharesTheHostNetworkNamespace",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Pod shares the host network namespace"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Deployment shop/storefront runs with hostNetwork: true. The pod shares the node's network namespace, so it can sniff traffic destined for other pods on the node, bind to privileged node ports, bypass NetworkPolicy entirely (policies do not apply to host-network pods), and reach the kubelet on localhost:10250."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Deployment shop/storefront runs with hostNetwork: true. The pod shares the node's network namespace, so it can sniff traffic destined for other pods on the node, bind to privileged node ports, bypass NetworkPolicy entirely (policies do not apply to host-network pods), and reach the kubelet on localhost:10250. **Remediation:** Use the pod network and expose the workload through a Service. ```yaml spec: hostNetwork: false dnsPolicy: ClusterFirst ``` CWE: CWE-668 \u00b7 MITRE: T1040, T1610 \u00b7 CIS: 5.2.4 \u00b7 NIST: SC-7",
                "markdown": "Deployment shop/storefront runs with hostNetwork: true. The pod shares the node's network namespace, so it can sniff traffic destined for other pods on the node, bind to privileged node ports, bypass NetworkPolicy entirely (policies do not apply to host-network pods), and reach the kubelet on localhost:10250.\n\n**Remediation:** Use the pod network and expose the workload through a Service.\n\n```yaml\nspec:\n  hostNetwork: false\n  dnsPolicy: ClusterFirst\n```\n\nCWE: CWE-668 \u00b7 MITRE: T1040, T1610 \u00b7 CIS: 5.2.4 \u00b7 NIST: SC-7"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
