            {
              "id": "TF-DATA-003",
              "name": "SecretIsReachableFromAnInternetFacingWorkload",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Secret is reachable from an internet-facing workload"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Secret shop/shop-db is only 4 hops from the internet along the data-flow graph. An RCE in the internet-facing component in that chain yields this secret directly. This is not a misconfiguration on its own -- it is a statement about blast radius that should drive where you invest in detection."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Secret shop/shop-db is only 4 hops from the internet along the data-flow graph. An RCE in the internet-facing component in that chain yields this secret directly. This is not a misconfiguration on its own -- it is a statement about blast radius that should drive where you invest in detection. **Remediation:** Shorten the trust chain -- scope the secret, or broker access through a short-lived token. Prefer workload identity (IRSA, Workload Identity, SPIFFE) over long-lived secrets. Where a static secret is unavoidable, scope it to the single consumer and rotate on a schedule short enough that theft has a bounded window. CWE: CWE-522 \u00b7 MITRE: T1552 \u00b7 NIST: SC-28, AC-6",
                "markdown": "Secret shop/shop-db is only 4 hops from the internet along the data-flow graph. An RCE in the internet-facing component in that chain yields this secret directly. This is not a misconfiguration on its own -- it is a statement about blast radius that should drive where you invest in detection.\n\n**Remediation:** Shorten the trust chain -- scope the secret, or broker access through a short-lived token.\n\nPrefer workload identity (IRSA, Workload Identity, SPIFFE) over long-lived secrets. Where a static secret is unavoidable, scope it to the single consumer and rotate on a schedule short enough that theft has a bounded window.\n\n\nCWE: CWE-522 \u00b7 MITRE: T1552 \u00b7 NIST: SC-28, AC-6"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "blast-radius",
                  "cwe/CWE-522",
                  "mitre/T1552",
                  "pack:k8s-data",
                  "secrets",
                  "security",
                  "stride/I"
                ],
                "security-severity": "6.0",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-DKR-003",
              "name": "SecretMaterialInBuildInstructions",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Secret material in build instructions"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "DockerImage node:latest embeds credential-shaped values in ENV/ARG/RUN. Docker layers are immutable and independently pullable: deleting the value in a later layer does not remove it, and anyone who can pull the image can extract it with `docker history` or by unpacking the layer tarballs."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "DockerImage node:latest embeds credential-shaped values in ENV/ARG/RUN. Docker layers are immutable and independently pullable: deleting the value in a later layer does not remove it, and anyone who can pull the image can extract it with `docker history` or by unpacking the layer tarballs. **Remediation:** Rotate the credential and use BuildKit secret mounts. ```yaml # syntax=docker/dockerfile:1.7 RUN --mount=type=secret,id=npmrc,target=/root/.npmrc \\ npm ci --omit=dev ``` CWE: CWE-798, CWE-540 \u00b7 MITRE: T1552.001 \u00b7 CIS: 4.10 \u00b7 NIST: IA-5",
                "markdown": "DockerImage node:latest embeds credential-shaped values in ENV/ARG/RUN. Docker layers are immutable and independently pullable: deleting the value in a later layer does not remove it, and anyone who can pull the image can extract it with `docker history` or by unpacking the layer tarballs.\n\n**Remediation:** Rotate the credential and use BuildKit secret mounts.\n\n```yaml\n# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1.7\nRUN --mount=type=secret,id=npmrc,target=/root/.npmrc \\\n    npm ci --omit=dev\n```\n\nCWE: CWE-798, CWE-540 \u00b7 MITRE: T1552.001 \u00b7 CIS: 4.10 \u00b7 NIST: IA-5"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "build",
                  "critical-path",
                  "cwe/CWE-540",
                  "cwe/CWE-798",
                  "mitre/T1552.001",
                  "pack:docker-build",
                  "secrets",
                  "security",
                  "stride/I"
                ],
                "security-severity": "6.0",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-K8S-009",
              "name": "ContainerDoesNotDropAllCapabilities",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Container does not drop all capabilities"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Container shop/postgres keeps the default Docker capability set (CHOWN, DAC_OVERRIDE, FOWNER, SETUID, SETGID, NET_RAW and others). SETUID/SETGID enable local privilege escalation via setuid binaries; NET_RAW enables ARP/DNS spoofing against pods sharing the node network."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Container shop/postgres keeps the default Docker capability set (CHOWN, DAC_OVERRIDE, FOWNER, SETUID, SETGID, NET_RAW and others). SETUID/SETGID enable local privilege escalation via setuid binaries; NET_RAW enables ARP/DNS spoofing against pods sharing the node network. **Remediation:** Drop ALL capabilities as the baseline. ```yaml securityContext: capabilities: drop: [\"ALL\"] ``` CWE: CWE-250 \u00b7 MITRE: T1548 \u00b7 CIS: 5.2.9 \u00b7 NIST: CM-7",
                "markdown": "Container shop/postgres keeps the default Docker capability set (CHOWN, DAC_OVERRIDE, FOWNER, SETUID, SETGID, NET_RAW and others). SETUID/SETGID enable local privilege escalation via setuid binaries; NET_RAW enables ARP/DNS spoofing against pods sharing the node network.\n\n**Remediation:** Drop ALL capabilities as the baseline.\n\n```yaml\nsecurityContext:\n  capabilities:\n    drop: [\"ALL\"]\n```\n\nCWE: CWE-250 \u00b7 MITRE: T1548 \u00b7 CIS: 5.2.9 \u00b7 NIST: CM-7"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "baseline",
                  "cwe/CWE-250",
                  "hardening",
                  "mitre/T1548",
                  "pack:k8s-workload",
                  "security",
                  "stride/E"
                ],
                "security-severity": "4.8",
                "precision": "very-high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-K8S-010",
              "name": "ContainerRootFilesystemIsWritable",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Container root filesystem is writable"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A writable root filesystem lets an attacker with code execution drop a web shell, overwrite application binaries, or install persistence that survives a process restart. It also removes a cheap tripwire: with a read-only root, most post-exploitation tooling fails immediately and noisily."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A writable root filesystem lets an attacker with code execution drop a web shell, overwrite application binaries, or install persistence that survives a process restart. It also removes a cheap tripwire: with a read-only root, most post-exploitation tooling fails immediately and noisily. **Remediation:** Make the root filesystem read-only and mount emptyDir for writable paths. ```yaml securityContext: readOnlyRootFilesystem: true volumeMounts: - name: tmp mountPath: /tmp # volumes: # - name: tmp # emptyDir: {} ``` CWE: CWE-732 \u00b7 MITRE: T1505.003, T1543 \u00b7 CIS: 5.2.11 \u00b7 NIST: SI-7",
                "markdown": "A writable root filesystem lets an attacker with code execution drop a web shell, overwrite application binaries, or install persistence that survives a process restart. It also removes a cheap tripwire: with a read-only root, most post-exploitation tooling fails immediately and noisily.\n\n**Remediation:** Make the root filesystem read-only and mount emptyDir for writable paths.\n\n```yaml\nsecurityContext:\n  readOnlyRootFilesystem: true\nvolumeMounts:\n  - name: tmp\n    mountPath: /tmp\n# volumes:\n#   - name: tmp\n#     emptyDir: {}\n```\n\nCWE: CWE-732 \u00b7 MITRE: T1505.003, T1543 \u00b7 CIS: 5.2.11 \u00b7 NIST: SI-7"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "cwe/CWE-732",
                  "hardening",
                  "mitre/T1505.003",
                  "mitre/T1543",
                  "pack:k8s-workload",
                  "persistence",
                  "security",
                  "stride/T"
                ],
                "security-severity": "4.8",
                "precision": "very-high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-K8S-013",
              "name": "ContainerHasNoCpuOrMemoryLimits",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Container has no CPU or memory limits"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Without resource limits, Container shop/postgres can consume all allocatable CPU and memory on its node. A memory leak or a deliberately expensive request becomes a node-wide outage: the kubelet begins evicting neighbouring pods, and a single compromised workload becomes a cluster-wide denial of service primitive."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Without resource limits, Container shop/postgres can consume all allocatable CPU and memory on its node. A memory leak or a deliberately expensive request becomes a node-wide outage: the kubelet begins evicting neighbouring pods, and a single compromised workload becomes a cluster-wide denial of service primitive. **Remediation:** Set requests and limits; enforce defaults with a LimitRange. ```yaml resources: requests: {cpu: \"100m\", memory: \"128Mi\"} limits: {cpu: \"500m\", memory: \"512Mi\"} ``` CWE: CWE-770, CWE-400 \u00b7 MITRE: T1499 \u00b7 CIS: 5.7.3 \u00b7 NIST: SC-5 \u00b7 OWASP: K07",
                "markdown": "Without resource limits, Container shop/postgres can consume all allocatable CPU and memory on its node. A memory leak or a deliberately expensive request becomes a node-wide outage: the kubelet begins evicting neighbouring pods, and a single compromised workload becomes a cluster-wide denial of service primitive.\n\n**Remediation:** Set requests and limits; enforce defaults with a LimitRange.\n\n```yaml\nresources:\n  requests: {cpu: \"100m\", memory: \"128Mi\"}\n  limits:   {cpu: \"500m\", memory: \"512Mi\"}\n```\n\nCWE: CWE-770, CWE-400 \u00b7 MITRE: T1499 \u00b7 CIS: 5.7.3 \u00b7 NIST: SC-5 \u00b7 OWASP: K07"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "availability",
                  "cwe/CWE-400",
                  "cwe/CWE-770",
                  "mitre/T1499",
                  "pack:k8s-workload",
                  "security",
                  "stride/D"
                ],
                "security-severity": "4.8",
                "precision": "very-high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-K8S-014",
              "name": "PodDoesNotSetASeccompProfile",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Pod does not set a seccomp profile"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "No seccomp profile is applied, so the container may issue all ~350 syscalls. Most container escapes published in the last several years relied on syscalls that RuntimeDefault blocks outright."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "No seccomp profile is applied, so the container may issue all ~350 syscalls. Most container escapes published in the last several years relied on syscalls that RuntimeDefault blocks outright. **Remediation:** Apply the RuntimeDefault seccomp profile. ```yaml spec: securityContext: seccompProfile: type: RuntimeDefault ``` CWE: CWE-693 \u00b7 MITRE: T1611 \u00b7 CIS: 5.7.2 \u00b7 NIST: SI-16, CM-7",
                "markdown": "No seccomp profile is applied, so the container may issue all ~350 syscalls. Most container escapes published in the last several years relied on syscalls that RuntimeDefault blocks outright.\n\n**Remediation:** Apply the RuntimeDefault seccomp profile.\n\n```yaml\nspec:\n  securityContext:\n    seccompProfile:\n      type: RuntimeDefault\n```\n\nCWE: CWE-693 \u00b7 MITRE: T1611 \u00b7 CIS: 5.7.2 \u00b7 NIST: SI-16, CM-7"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "baseline",
                  "cwe/CWE-693",
                  "hardening",
                  "mitre/T1611",
                  "pack:k8s-workload",
                  "security",
                  "stride/E"
                ],
                "security-severity": "4.8",
                "precision": "very-high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-NET-002",
              "name": "WorkloadHasNoEgressNetworkpolicy",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Workload has no egress NetworkPolicy"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "StatefulSet shop/postgres has unrestricted egress. That is the exfiltration path and the command-and-control path: a compromised pod can reach any external host, any internal service, and the cloud metadata endpoint at 169.254.169.254 to steal node IAM credentials."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "StatefulSet shop/postgres has unrestricted egress. That is the exfiltration path and the command-and-control path: a compromised pod can reach any external host, any internal service, and the cloud metadata endpoint at 169.254.169.254 to steal node IAM credentials. **Remediation:** Default-deny egress; allow DNS plus the specific destinations required. ```yaml apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: NetworkPolicy metadata: name: default-deny-egress namespace: {{ namespace }} spec: podSelector: {} policyTypes: [\"Egress\"] egress: - to: - namespaceSelector: {matchLabels: {kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kube-system}} ports: [{protocol: UDP, port: 53}] - to: - ipBlock: cidr: 0.0.0.0/0 except: [\"169.254.169.254/32\", \"10.0.0.0/8\"] ``` CWE: CWE-1327 \u00b7 MITRE: T1041, T1552.005 \u00b7 CIS: 5.3.2 \u00b7 NIST: SC-7, AC-4 \u00b7 OWASP: K05",
                "markdown": "StatefulSet shop/postgres has unrestricted egress. That is the exfiltration path and the command-and-control path: a compromised pod can reach any external host, any internal service, and the cloud metadata endpoint at 169.254.169.254 to steal node IAM credentials.\n\n**Remediation:** Default-deny egress; allow DNS plus the specific destinations required.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: NetworkPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: default-deny-egress\n  namespace: {{ namespace }}\nspec:\n  podSelector: {}\n  policyTypes: [\"Egress\"]\n  egress:\n    - to:\n        - namespaceSelector: {matchLabels: {kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kube-system}}\n      ports: [{protocol: UDP, port: 53}]\n    - to:\n        - ipBlock:\n            cidr: 0.0.0.0/0\n            except: [\"169.254.169.254/32\", \"10.0.0.0/8\"]\n```\n\nCWE: CWE-1327 \u00b7 MITRE: T1041, T1552.005 \u00b7 CIS: 5.3.2 \u00b7 NIST: SC-7, AC-4 \u00b7 OWASP: K05"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "cwe/CWE-1327",
                  "exfiltration",
                  "mitre/T1041",
                  "mitre/T1552.005",
                  "network",
                  "pack:k8s-network",
                  "security",
                  "stride/E",
                  "stride/I"
                ],
                "security-severity": "4.8",
                "precision": "very-high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-NET-005",
              "name": "InternetFacingIngressHasNoAuthenticationOrRateLimiting",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Internet-facing Ingress has no authentication or rate limiting"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Ingress shop/storefront-ingress is exposed to the internet with no ingress-level authentication and no rate limit. Whether that is acceptable depends on whether the backend authenticates -- but the absence of a rate limit is unconditionally a denial of service and credential-stuffing exposure."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Ingress shop/storefront-ingress is exposed to the internet with no ingress-level authentication and no rate limit. Whether that is acceptable depends on whether the backend authenticates -- but the absence of a rate limit is unconditionally a denial of service and credential-stuffing exposure. **Remediation:** Add rate limiting at the ingress; add edge authentication for non-public endpoints. ```yaml metadata: annotations: nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/limit-rps: \"20\" nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/limit-connections: \"10\" # for internal tools: # nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/auth-url: \"https://oauth2-proxy.example.com/oauth2/auth\" ``` CWE: CWE-307, CWE-770 \u00b7 MITRE: T1110, T1499 \u00b7 NIST: SC-5, IA-2 \u00b7 OWASP: K07",
                "markdown": "Ingress shop/storefront-ingress is exposed to the internet with no ingress-level authentication and no rate limit. Whether that is acceptable depends on whether the backend authenticates -- but the absence of a rate limit is unconditionally a denial of service and credential-stuffing exposure.\n\n**Remediation:** Add rate limiting at the ingress; add edge authentication for non-public endpoints.\n\n```yaml\nmetadata:\n  annotations:\n    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/limit-rps: \"20\"\n    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/limit-connections: \"10\"\n    # for internal tools:\n    # nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/auth-url: \"https://oauth2-proxy.example.com/oauth2/auth\"\n```\n\nCWE: CWE-307, CWE-770 \u00b7 MITRE: T1110, T1499 \u00b7 NIST: SC-5, IA-2 \u00b7 OWASP: K07"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "authentication",
                  "availability",
                  "cwe/CWE-307",
                  "cwe/CWE-770",
                  "mitre/T1110",
                  "mitre/T1499",
                  "pack:k8s-network",
                  "security",
                  "stride/D",
                  "stride/S"
                ],
                "security-severity": "4.8",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-NET-006",
              "name": "IngressUsesAWildcardHost",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Ingress uses a wildcard host"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A wildcard host binds this backend to every subdomain, which enables subdomain takeover chains and makes cookie scoping decisions unsafe -- a cookie set on *.example.com is readable by every other service under it."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A wildcard host binds this backend to every subdomain, which enables subdomain takeover chains and makes cookie scoping decisions unsafe -- a cookie set on *.example.com is readable by every other service under it. **Remediation:** List explicit hostnames. CWE: CWE-346 \u00b7 MITRE: T1584.001 \u00b7 NIST: SC-7",
                "markdown": "A wildcard host binds this backend to every subdomain, which enables subdomain takeover chains and makes cookie scoping decisions unsafe -- a cookie set on *.example.com is readable by every other service under it.\n\n**Remediation:** List explicit hostnames.\n\nCWE: CWE-346 \u00b7 MITRE: T1584.001 \u00b7 NIST: SC-7"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "cwe/CWE-346",
                  "mitre/T1584.001",
                  "network",
                  "pack:k8s-network",
                  "security",
                  "stride/S",
                  "stride/T"
                ],
                "security-severity": "4.8",
                "precision": "very-high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-RBAC-005",
              "name": "RoleCanWriteSecrets",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Role can write Secrets"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Write access to Secrets allows an attacker to plant credentials that other workloads will trust -- for example replacing a TLS keypair or an image pull secret to redirect pulls to an attacker-controlled registry."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Write access to Secrets allows an attacker to plant credentials that other workloads will trust -- for example replacing a TLS keypair or an image pull secret to redirect pulls to an attacker-controlled registry. **Remediation:** Remove write access; provision Secrets through a controller instead. CWE: CWE-732 \u00b7 MITRE: T1098, T1552 \u00b7 NIST: AC-6, SC-28",
                "markdown": "Write access to Secrets allows an attacker to plant credentials that other workloads will trust -- for example replacing a TLS keypair or an image pull secret to redirect pulls to an attacker-controlled registry.\n\n**Remediation:** Remove write access; provision Secrets through a controller instead.\n\nCWE: CWE-732 \u00b7 MITRE: T1098, T1552 \u00b7 NIST: AC-6, SC-28"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "cwe/CWE-732",
                  "mitre/T1098",
                  "mitre/T1552",
                  "pack:k8s-identity",
                  "rbac",
                  "secrets",
                  "security",
                  "stride/E",
                  "stride/T"
                ],
                "security-severity": "4.8",
                "precision": "very-high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-CLOUD-009",
              "name": "ComputeInstancePermitsImdsv1",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Compute instance permits IMDSv1"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "aws_instance worker allows IMDSv1. An SSRF in any application on the instance reaches 169.254.169.254 with a single unauthenticated GET and returns the instance role's temporary credentials. IMDSv2's PUT-token requirement blocks the entire SSRF class."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "aws_instance worker allows IMDSv1. An SSRF in any application on the instance reaches 169.254.169.254 with a single unauthenticated GET and returns the instance role's temporary credentials. IMDSv2's PUT-token requirement blocks the entire SSRF class. **Remediation:** Require IMDSv2 and lower the hop limit. ```yaml metadata_options { http_tokens = \"required\" http_endpoint = \"enabled\" http_put_response_hop_limit = 1 } ``` CWE: CWE-918, CWE-522 \u00b7 MITRE: T1552.005 \u00b7 NIST: SC-7, IA-5",
                "markdown": "aws_instance worker allows IMDSv1. An SSRF in any application on the instance reaches 169.254.169.254 with a single unauthenticated GET and returns the instance role's temporary credentials. IMDSv2's PUT-token requirement blocks the entire SSRF class.\n\n**Remediation:** Require IMDSv2 and lower the hop limit.\n\n```yaml\nmetadata_options {\n  http_tokens                 = \"required\"\n  http_endpoint               = \"enabled\"\n  http_put_response_hop_limit = 1\n}\n```\n\nCWE: CWE-918, CWE-522 \u00b7 MITRE: T1552.005 \u00b7 NIST: SC-7, IA-5"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "cloud",
                  "critical-path",
                  "cwe/CWE-522",
                  "cwe/CWE-918",
                  "mitre/T1552.005",
                  "pack:cloud-terraform",
                  "security",
                  "ssrf",
                  "stride/E",
                  "stride/I"
                ],
                "security-severity": "4.0",
                "precision": "very-high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-DKR-001",
              "name": "ImageRunsAsRoot",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Image runs as root"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The final stage of DockerImage node:latest has no USER instruction, so the image runs as UID 0. Every downstream consumer inherits that default -- an orchestrator has to opt out explicitly, and most manifests never do."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The final stage of DockerImage node:latest has no USER instruction, so the image runs as UID 0. Every downstream consumer inherits that default -- an orchestrator has to opt out explicitly, and most manifests never do. **Remediation:** Create and switch to a non-root user. ```yaml RUN addgroup -g 10001 app && adduser -D -u 10001 -G app app USER 10001:10001 ``` CWE: CWE-250 \u00b7 MITRE: T1610 \u00b7 CIS: 4.1 \u00b7 NIST: AC-6",
                "markdown": "The final stage of DockerImage node:latest has no USER instruction, so the image runs as UID 0. Every downstream consumer inherits that default -- an orchestrator has to opt out explicitly, and most manifests never do.\n\n**Remediation:** Create and switch to a non-root user.\n\n```yaml\nRUN addgroup -g 10001 app && adduser -D -u 10001 -G app app\nUSER 10001:10001\n```\n\nCWE: CWE-250 \u00b7 MITRE: T1610 \u00b7 CIS: 4.1 \u00b7 NIST: AC-6"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "build",
                  "cwe/CWE-250",
                  "mitre/T1610",
                  "pack:docker-build",
                  "privilege",
                  "security",
                  "stride/E"
                ],
                "security-severity": "4.0",
                "precision": "very-high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-DKR-005",
              "name": "BuildPipesRemoteScriptDirectlyIntoAShell",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Build pipes remote script directly into a shell"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A `curl ... | sh` pattern executes unverified remote code as root during the build. Whoever controls that URL controls the contents of your image, and the compromise leaves no trace in the Dockerfile."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A `curl ... | sh` pattern executes unverified remote code as root during the build. Whoever controls that URL controls the contents of your image, and the compromise leaves no trace in the Dockerfile. **Remediation:** Download, verify the checksum, inspect, then execute. CWE: CWE-494 \u00b7 MITRE: T1195.002, T1059 \u00b7 NIST: SI-7, CM-7",
                "markdown": "A `curl ... | sh` pattern executes unverified remote code as root during the build. Whoever controls that URL controls the contents of your image, and the compromise leaves no trace in the Dockerfile.\n\n**Remediation:** Download, verify the checksum, inspect, then execute.\n\nCWE: CWE-494 \u00b7 MITRE: T1195.002, T1059 \u00b7 NIST: SI-7, CM-7"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "build",
                  "cwe/CWE-494",
                  "mitre/T1059",
                  "mitre/T1195.002",
                  "pack:docker-build",
                  "security",
                  "stride/E",
                  "stride/T",
                  "supply-chain"
                ],
                "security-severity": "4.0",
                "precision": "very-high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-CMP-005",
              "name": "ComposeServiceHasNoResourceLimits",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Compose service has no resource limits"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "No CPU or memory limit is set, so this container can exhaust the host."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "No CPU or memory limit is set, so this container can exhaust the host. **Remediation:** Set deploy.resources.limits. ```yaml deploy: resources: limits: {cpus: \"0.50\", memory: 512M} ``` CWE: CWE-770 \u00b7 MITRE: T1499 \u00b7 NIST: SC-5",
                "markdown": "No CPU or memory limit is set, so this container can exhaust the host.\n\n**Remediation:** Set deploy.resources.limits.\n\n```yaml\ndeploy:\n  resources:\n    limits: {cpus: \"0.50\", memory: 512M}\n```\n\nCWE: CWE-770 \u00b7 MITRE: T1499 \u00b7 NIST: SC-5"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "availability",
                  "compose",
                  "cwe/CWE-770",
                  "mitre/T1499",
                  "pack:docker-build",
                  "security",
                  "stride/D"
                ],
                "security-severity": "3.6",
                "precision": "very-high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-FLOW-002",
              "name": "SensitiveDataCrossesATrustBoundary",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Sensitive data crosses a trust boundary"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Deployment shop/storefront -> ExternalEntity Stripe (payment provider) moves data classified as pci across a trust boundary. Each boundary crossing is a place where authentication, authorisation, encryption, and logging all need to be re-established -- assumptions that held on one side rarely hold on the other."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Deployment shop/storefront -> ExternalEntity Stripe (payment provider) moves data classified as pci across a trust boundary. Each boundary crossing is a place where authentication, authorisation, encryption, and logging all need to be re-established -- assumptions that held on one side rarely hold on the other. **Remediation:** Enforce mTLS and explicit authorisation on this hop; log both sides. A service mesh (Istio, Linkerd) gives mTLS and per-hop authorisation policy without application changes, and produces the flow-level telemetry you need to detect abuse of this path. CWE: CWE-311, CWE-306 \u00b7 MITRE: T1557, T1041 \u00b7 NIST: SC-8, AC-4",
                "markdown": "Deployment shop/storefront -> ExternalEntity Stripe (payment provider) moves data classified as pci across a trust boundary. Each boundary crossing is a place where authentication, authorisation, encryption, and logging all need to be re-established -- assumptions that held on one side rarely hold on the other.\n\n**Remediation:** Enforce mTLS and explicit authorisation on this hop; log both sides.\n\nA service mesh (Istio, Linkerd) gives mTLS and per-hop authorisation policy without application changes, and produces the flow-level telemetry you need to detect abuse of this path.\n\n\nCWE: CWE-311, CWE-306 \u00b7 MITRE: T1557, T1041 \u00b7 NIST: SC-8, AC-4"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "boundary",
                  "cwe/CWE-306",
                  "cwe/CWE-311",
                  "encryption",
                  "mitre/T1041",
                  "mitre/T1557",
                  "pack:dataflow",
                  "security",
                  "stride/I",
                  "stride/T"
                ],
                "security-severity": "3.6",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-K8S-011",
              "name": "ContainerImageUsesAMutableTag",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Container image uses a mutable tag"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Container shop/web references image `shop/storefront:latest` by a mutable tag. What runs in production is therefore whatever the registry served at pull time. This breaks reproducibility, defeats admission-time image scanning, and makes supply chain tampering hard to detect -- an attacker who can push to the registry can replace the image without any manifest change."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Container shop/web references image `shop/storefront:latest` by a mutable tag. What runs in production is therefore whatever the registry served at pull time. This breaks reproducibility, defeats admission-time image scanning, and makes supply chain tampering hard to detect -- an attacker who can push to the registry can replace the image without any manifest change. **Remediation:** Pin images by digest. ```yaml image: myrepo/myapp@sha256:0f6a... # digest, not :latest imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent ``` CWE: CWE-494, CWE-829 \u00b7 MITRE: T1525, T1195.002 \u00b7 CIS: 5.1.4 \u00b7 NIST: SI-7, CM-2 \u00b7 OWASP: K06",
                "markdown": "Container shop/web references image `shop/storefront:latest` by a mutable tag. What runs in production is therefore whatever the registry served at pull time. This breaks reproducibility, defeats admission-time image scanning, and makes supply chain tampering hard to detect -- an attacker who can push to the registry can replace the image without any manifest change.\n\n**Remediation:** Pin images by digest.\n\n```yaml\nimage: myrepo/myapp@sha256:0f6a...   # digest, not :latest\nimagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent\n```\n\nCWE: CWE-494, CWE-829 \u00b7 MITRE: T1525, T1195.002 \u00b7 CIS: 5.1.4 \u00b7 NIST: SI-7, CM-2 \u00b7 OWASP: K06"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "cwe/CWE-494",
                  "cwe/CWE-829",
                  "mitre/T1195.002",
                  "mitre/T1525",
                  "pack:k8s-workload",
                  "security",
                  "stride/S",
                  "stride/T",
                  "supply-chain"
                ],
                "security-severity": "3.6",
                "precision": "very-high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-K8S-016",
              "name": "WorkloadUsesTheDefaultServiceAccount",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Workload uses the default service account"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Deployment shop/storefront runs as the namespace `default` service account with its token automounted. The default SA is shared by every workload in the namespace, so any permission granted to it is granted to all of them, and token theft cannot be attributed to a specific workload."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Deployment shop/storefront runs as the namespace `default` service account with its token automounted. The default SA is shared by every workload in the namespace, so any permission granted to it is granted to all of them, and token theft cannot be attributed to a specific workload. **Remediation:** Create a dedicated ServiceAccount per workload; disable automount if the API is not used. ```yaml spec: serviceAccountName: myapp automountServiceAccountToken: false # if the pod never calls the API server ``` CWE: CWE-250, CWE-269 \u00b7 MITRE: T1528, T1078.004 \u00b7 CIS: 5.1.5, 5.1.6 \u00b7 NIST: AC-2, AC-6 \u00b7 OWASP: K03",
                "markdown": "Deployment shop/storefront runs as the namespace `default` service account with its token automounted. The default SA is shared by every workload in the namespace, so any permission granted to it is granted to all of them, and token theft cannot be attributed to a specific workload.\n\n**Remediation:** Create a dedicated ServiceAccount per workload; disable automount if the API is not used.\n\n```yaml\nspec:\n  serviceAccountName: myapp\n  automountServiceAccountToken: false   # if the pod never calls the API server\n```\n\nCWE: CWE-250, CWE-269 \u00b7 MITRE: T1528, T1078.004 \u00b7 CIS: 5.1.5, 5.1.6 \u00b7 NIST: AC-2, AC-6 \u00b7 OWASP: K03"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "baseline",
                  "cwe/CWE-250",
                  "cwe/CWE-269",
                  "identity",
                  "mitre/T1078.004",
                  "mitre/T1528",
                  "pack:k8s-workload",
                  "security",
                  "stride/E",
                  "stride/S"
                ],
                "security-severity": "3.6",
                "precision": "very-high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-K8S-018",
              "name": "ContainerBindsAHostPort",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Container binds a host port"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Container shop/web binds host port(s) 8080. The service is reachable on every node IP, bypassing Service-level controls and NetworkPolicy, and it constrains scheduling in ways that make node-level firewalling brittle."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Container shop/web binds host port(s) 8080. The service is reachable on every node IP, bypassing Service-level controls and NetworkPolicy, and it constrains scheduling in ways that make node-level firewalling brittle. **Remediation:** Expose through a Service and an Ingress instead of a host port. CWE: CWE-668 \u00b7 MITRE: T1571 \u00b7 CIS: 5.2.4 \u00b7 NIST: SC-7",
                "markdown": "Container shop/web binds host port(s) 8080. The service is reachable on every node IP, bypassing Service-level controls and NetworkPolicy, and it constrains scheduling in ways that make node-level firewalling brittle.\n\n**Remediation:** Expose through a Service and an Ingress instead of a host port.\n\nCWE: CWE-668 \u00b7 MITRE: T1571 \u00b7 CIS: 5.2.4 \u00b7 NIST: SC-7"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "cwe/CWE-668",
                  "mitre/T1571",
                  "network",
                  "pack:k8s-workload",
                  "security",
                  "stride/D",
                  "stride/I",
                  "stride/S"
                ],
                "security-severity": "3.6",
                "precision": "very-high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-RBAC-007",
              "name": "ServiceAccountTokenAutomountedOnTheDefaultServiceAccount",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Service account token automounted on the default service account"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The `default` ServiceAccount in namespace shop automounts its token into every pod that does not specify another SA. This is the most commonly abused foothold for lateral movement inside a namespace."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The `default` ServiceAccount in namespace shop automounts its token into every pod that does not specify another SA. This is the most commonly abused foothold for lateral movement inside a namespace. **Remediation:** Disable automount on the default service account. ```yaml apiVersion: v1 kind: ServiceAccount metadata: name: default namespace: {{ namespace }} automountServiceAccountToken: false ``` CWE: CWE-522 \u00b7 MITRE: T1528 \u00b7 CIS: 5.1.5 \u00b7 NIST: AC-6",
                "markdown": "The `default` ServiceAccount in namespace shop automounts its token into every pod that does not specify another SA. This is the most commonly abused foothold for lateral movement inside a namespace.\n\n**Remediation:** Disable automount on the default service account.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: v1\nkind: ServiceAccount\nmetadata:\n  name: default\n  namespace: {{ namespace }}\nautomountServiceAccountToken: false\n```\n\nCWE: CWE-522 \u00b7 MITRE: T1528 \u00b7 CIS: 5.1.5 \u00b7 NIST: AC-6"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "baseline",
                  "cwe/CWE-522",
                  "identity",
                  "mitre/T1528",
                  "pack:k8s-identity",
                  "security",
                  "stride/E"
                ],
                "security-severity": "3.6",
                "precision": "very-high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-CLOUD-003",
              "name": "SecurityGroupAllowsIngressFrom00000",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Security group allows ingress from 0.0.0.0/0"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "aws_security_group web permits inbound traffic from the entire internet on port(s) 22. If any of those are 22, 3389, 3306, 5432, or 6379 this is immediately exploitable; even for 443 it removes the option of IP-based containment during an incident."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "aws_security_group web permits inbound traffic from the entire internet on port(s) 22. If any of those are 22, 3389, 3306, 5432, or 6379 this is immediately exploitable; even for 443 it removes the option of IP-based containment during an incident. **Remediation:** Restrict to known CIDRs, or front the service with a load balancer and WAF. ```yaml ingress { from_port = 443 to_port = 443 protocol = \"tcp\" cidr_blocks = [\"203.0.113.0/24\"] } ``` CWE: CWE-284 \u00b7 MITRE: T1190, T1133 \u00b7 NIST: SC-7, AC-4",
                "markdown": "aws_security_group web permits inbound traffic from the entire internet on port(s) 22. If any of those are 22, 3389, 3306, 5432, or 6379 this is immediately exploitable; even for 443 it removes the option of IP-based containment during an incident.\n\n**Remediation:** Restrict to known CIDRs, or front the service with a load balancer and WAF.\n\n```yaml\ningress {\n  from_port   = 443\n  to_port     = 443\n  protocol    = \"tcp\"\n  cidr_blocks = [\"203.0.113.0/24\"]\n}\n```\n\nCWE: CWE-284 \u00b7 MITRE: T1190, T1133 \u00b7 NIST: SC-7, AC-4"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "cloud",
                  "cwe/CWE-284",
                  "mitre/T1133",
                  "mitre/T1190",
                  "network",
                  "pack:cloud-terraform",
                  "security",
                  "stride/D",
                  "stride/E",
                  "stride/S"
                ],
                "security-severity": "3.2",
                "precision": "very-high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-CLOUD-004",
              "name": "DataStoreHasEncryptionAtRestDisabled",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Data store has encryption at rest disabled"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "aws_db_instance shop stores data unencrypted at rest. Snapshot sharing, disk decommissioning, and support access all become disclosure paths, and most compliance regimes treat this as a reportable control failure on its own."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "aws_db_instance shop stores data unencrypted at rest. Snapshot sharing, disk decommissioning, and support access all become disclosure paths, and most compliance regimes treat this as a reportable control failure on its own. **Remediation:** Enable encryption with a customer-managed KMS key. ```yaml storage_encrypted = true kms_key_id = aws_kms_key.data.arn ``` CWE: CWE-311 \u00b7 MITRE: T1530 \u00b7 NIST: SC-28",
                "markdown": "aws_db_instance shop stores data unencrypted at rest. Snapshot sharing, disk decommissioning, and support access all become disclosure paths, and most compliance regimes treat this as a reportable control failure on its own.\n\n**Remediation:** Enable encryption with a customer-managed KMS key.\n\n```yaml\nstorage_encrypted = true\nkms_key_id        = aws_kms_key.data.arn\n```\n\nCWE: CWE-311 \u00b7 MITRE: T1530 \u00b7 NIST: SC-28"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "cloud",
                  "cwe/CWE-311",
                  "encryption",
                  "mitre/T1530",
                  "pack:cloud-terraform",
                  "security",
                  "stride/I"
                ],
                "security-severity": "3.2",
                "precision": "very-high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-CMP-006",
              "name": "ComposeServiceUsesHostNetworking",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Compose service uses host networking"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "network_mode host removes network namespace isolation: the container can bind any host port and sniff all host traffic."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "network_mode host removes network namespace isolation: the container can bind any host port and sniff all host traffic. **Remediation:** Use a user-defined bridge network and publish only required ports. CWE: CWE-668 \u00b7 MITRE: T1040 \u00b7 NIST: SC-7",
                "markdown": "network_mode host removes network namespace isolation: the container can bind any host port and sniff all host traffic.\n\n**Remediation:** Use a user-defined bridge network and publish only required ports.\n\nCWE: CWE-668 \u00b7 MITRE: T1040 \u00b7 NIST: SC-7"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compose",
                  "cwe/CWE-668",
                  "mitre/T1040",
                  "network",
                  "pack:docker-build",
                  "security",
                  "stride/I",
                  "stride/S"
                ],
                "security-severity": "3.2",
                "precision": "very-high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-DATA-002",
              "name": "CredentialLikeValuesStoredInAConfigmap",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Credential-like values stored in a ConfigMap"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "ConfigMap shop/shop-config holds keys API_TOKEN that look like credentials. ConfigMaps get none of the (already modest) protections Secrets receive: they are not candidates for encryption at rest by default, are routinely granted broad read access, and are frequently dumped wholesale into logs and support bundles."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "ConfigMap shop/shop-config holds keys API_TOKEN that look like credentials. ConfigMaps get none of the (already modest) protections Secrets receive: they are not candidates for encryption at rest by default, are routinely granted broad read access, and are frequently dumped wholesale into logs and support bundles. **Remediation:** Move these keys into a Secret or an external secret store, and rotate them. CWE: CWE-522, CWE-312 \u00b7 MITRE: T1552.001 \u00b7 NIST: SC-28, IA-5 \u00b7 OWASP: K08",
                "markdown": "ConfigMap shop/shop-config holds keys API_TOKEN that look like credentials. ConfigMaps get none of the (already modest) protections Secrets receive: they are not candidates for encryption at rest by default, are routinely granted broad read access, and are frequently dumped wholesale into logs and support bundles.\n\n**Remediation:** Move these keys into a Secret or an external secret store, and rotate them.\n\nCWE: CWE-522, CWE-312 \u00b7 MITRE: T1552.001 \u00b7 NIST: SC-28, IA-5 \u00b7 OWASP: K08"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "cwe/CWE-312",
                  "cwe/CWE-522",
                  "mitre/T1552.001",
                  "pack:k8s-data",
                  "secrets",
                  "security",
                  "stride/I"
                ],
                "security-severity": "3.2",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-K8S-012",
              "name": "ContainerImageIsNotPinnedByDigest",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Container image is not pinned by digest"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Container shop/postgres uses a version tag rather than a digest. Version tags are still mutable in most registries, so this is weaker than it looks, though far better than :latest."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Container shop/postgres uses a version tag rather than a digest. Version tags are still mutable in most registries, so this is weaker than it looks, though far better than :latest. **Remediation:** Resolve the tag to a digest in CI and commit the digest. CWE: CWE-829 \u00b7 MITRE: T1195.002 \u00b7 NIST: CM-2",
                "markdown": "Container shop/postgres uses a version tag rather than a digest. Version tags are still mutable in most registries, so this is weaker than it looks, though far better than :latest.\n\n**Remediation:** Resolve the tag to a digest in CI and commit the digest.\n\nCWE: CWE-829 \u00b7 MITRE: T1195.002 \u00b7 NIST: CM-2"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "cwe/CWE-829",
                  "hygiene",
                  "mitre/T1195.002",
                  "pack:k8s-workload",
                  "security",
                  "stride/T",
                  "supply-chain"
                ],
                "security-severity": "2.4",
                "precision": "very-high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-K8S-019",
              "name": "WorkloadHasNoLivenessOrReadinessProbe",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Workload has no liveness or readiness probe"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Without probes, Kubernetes cannot tell a hung process from a healthy one. A deadlocked or resource-starved container keeps receiving traffic, which turns a partial failure into a user-visible outage and masks the early signal of an ongoing resource-exhaustion attack."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Without probes, Kubernetes cannot tell a hung process from a healthy one. A deadlocked or resource-starved container keeps receiving traffic, which turns a partial failure into a user-visible outage and masks the early signal of an ongoing resource-exhaustion attack. **Remediation:** Add liveness and readiness probes. ```yaml readinessProbe: httpGet: {path: /healthz, port: 8080} initialDelaySeconds: 5 livenessProbe: httpGet: {path: /healthz, port: 8080} initialDelaySeconds: 15 ``` CWE: CWE-703 \u00b7 MITRE: T1499 \u00b7 NIST: SI-4",
                "markdown": "Without probes, Kubernetes cannot tell a hung process from a healthy one. A deadlocked or resource-starved container keeps receiving traffic, which turns a partial failure into a user-visible outage and masks the early signal of an ongoing resource-exhaustion attack.\n\n**Remediation:** Add liveness and readiness probes.\n\n```yaml\nreadinessProbe:\n  httpGet: {path: /healthz, port: 8080}\n  initialDelaySeconds: 5\nlivenessProbe:\n  httpGet: {path: /healthz, port: 8080}\n  initialDelaySeconds: 15\n```\n\nCWE: CWE-703 \u00b7 MITRE: T1499 \u00b7 NIST: SI-4"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "availability",
                  "cwe/CWE-703",
                  "hygiene",
                  "mitre/T1499",
                  "pack:k8s-workload",
                  "security",
                  "stride/D"
                ],
                "security-severity": "2.4",
                "precision": "very-high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-K8S-020",
              "name": "SingleReplicaWorkloadIsASinglePointOfFailure",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Single-replica workload is a single point of failure"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "StatefulSet shop/postgres is internet-reachable but runs a single replica, so any crash, eviction, or node drain takes the service down. It also means a single successful resource-exhaustion request has full impact."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "StatefulSet shop/postgres is internet-reachable but runs a single replica, so any crash, eviction, or node drain takes the service down. It also means a single successful resource-exhaustion request has full impact. **Remediation:** Run at least two replicas with a PodDisruptionBudget and anti-affinity. ```yaml spec: replicas: 3 # plus a PodDisruptionBudget with minAvailable: 2 ``` CWE: CWE-1188 \u00b7 MITRE: T1499 \u00b7 NIST: CP-2",
                "markdown": "StatefulSet shop/postgres is internet-reachable but runs a single replica, so any crash, eviction, or node drain takes the service down. It also means a single successful resource-exhaustion request has full impact.\n\n**Remediation:** Run at least two replicas with a PodDisruptionBudget and anti-affinity.\n\n```yaml\nspec:\n  replicas: 3\n# plus a PodDisruptionBudget with minAvailable: 2\n```\n\nCWE: CWE-1188 \u00b7 MITRE: T1499 \u00b7 NIST: CP-2"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "availability",
                  "cwe/CWE-1188",
                  "mitre/T1499",
                  "pack:k8s-workload",
                  "security",
                  "stride/D"
                ],
                "security-severity": "2.4",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "TF-DKR-002",
              "name": "BaseImageUsesAMutableTag",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Base image uses a mutable tag"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "DockerImage node:latest builds FROM `node:latest`. Two builds of the same commit can produce different images, which breaks reproducibility and makes it impossible to say with confidence what is running in production."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "DockerImage node:latest builds FROM `node:latest`. Two builds of the same commit can produce different images, which breaks reproducibility and makes it impossible to say with confidence what is running in production. **Remediation:** Pin the base image by digest. ```yaml FROM alpine:3.20@sha256:0a4eaa0eecf5f8c050e5bba433f58c052be7587ee8af3e8b3910ef9ab5fbe9f5 ``` CWE: CWE-829, CWE-494 \u00b7 MITRE: T1195.002 \u00b7 CIS: 4.2 \u00b7 NIST: CM-2, SI-7",
                "markdown": "DockerImage node:latest builds FROM `node:latest`. Two builds of the same commit can produce different images, which breaks reproducibility and makes it impossible to say with confidence what is running in production.\n\n**Remediation:** Pin the base image by digest.\n\n```yaml\nFROM alpine:3.20@sha256:0a4eaa0eecf5f8c050e5bba433f58c052be7587ee8af3e8b3910ef9ab5fbe9f5\n```\n\nCWE: CWE-829, CWE-494 \u00b7 MITRE: T1195.002 \u00b7 CIS: 4.2 \u00b7 NIST: CM-2, SI-7"
